window.__saqPaperQuestions("2016-1-pah",[{"number":1,"title":"","stemText":"A 25 year old woman has been brought to the ED after a high speed head-on motor vehicle\naccident. Her initial chest x-ray is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Give the most important pathology demonstrated on this x-ray, with 3 radiographic supporting features. (4 marks) Most important pathology Radiographic supporting features"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What will be your definitive treatment of the diagnosis in Q1? (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What other clinically significant pathology is demonstrated in this X-ray? Include the radiographic support for this diagnosis. (2 marks) Pathology Radiographic supporting features You have managed the above injuries and have intubated the patient to optimise ongoing assessment and management. After a short period, the ventilator has begun to alarm due to high pressures."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List six possible causes of high ventilator pressures. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________\nSAQ 1 (continued)\nThe patient has undergone CT scanning to delineate her injuries. A slice from her head CT is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET."},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List the abnormalities seen on the image. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________ CT scanning has revealed only chest and head injuries as demonstrated in the above images. The chest injury has been satisfactorily managed. The patient has a dilated left pupil.\nHer vital signs are:\n\nGCS\nsedated and paralysed Pulse 100 /min\n\nBP\n90/60 mmHg O2 sats 90%\n\nFiO2 0.5"},{"id":"f","label":"Part F","prompt":"State your management steps, including drugs and doses and treatment end-points where appropriate. (8 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img3eaa4b229ae7341c82","asset:img32be62ef8706f64e46"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Left tension pneumothorax</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lung edge visible left upper lobe approx. 2cm in from chest</div><div class=\"answer-line\">wall</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Deep sulcus sign – basal air accumulation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Mediastinal shift to right</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">- aim Na approx. 150mmmol/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- normal glucose and temperature</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Mediastinal shift to right</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Insertion 32 Fr left ICC</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Left pulmonary contusion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Alveolar (fluffy will do) infiltrate left lower zone</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part F</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">- inadequately drained / recurrent tension pneumo</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- blocked ETT</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- ETT in right main bronchus / malpositioned tube</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- pt-ventilator dysynchrony</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- bronchospasm (asthma / anaphylaxis / aspiration)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- “over-ventilation” / dynamic hyper-inflation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">5.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- large left sided acute subdural haemorrhage (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- signs raised ICP (1 mark, plus 1 mark for each of below)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- midline shift to right</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- loss of sulci left</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- cerebral oedema (loss grey-white differentiation left side)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- compression left lateral ventricle</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- (accept subfalcine herniation)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">6. (1 mark for each)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- immediate neurosurgical referral for surgery</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- maintain MAP &gt;75mmHg with IV fluid bolus N/S 1000mL then noradrenaline infusion if needed</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- maintain O2 sats &gt;90% (increase FiO2)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- NeuroICU measures</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- Raised ICP strategy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- hyperventilate to get PCO2 30-35mmHg until operative management</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- bolus osmotherapy (accept 3% saline 5mL/kg or mannitol 1g/kg)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- aim Na approx. 150mmmol/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- normal glucose and temperature</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bold = essential to score &gt; 0; points for strategies and/or details</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":2,"title":"","stemText":"A 30 year old man has presented to the ED with a painful, swollen left foot after jumping\n• ver a high fence. There are no other injuries.\nHis x-rays are provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List five abnormalities on the x-ray. (5 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State your key management steps in the ED. Where appropriate, provide any doses\n• f medications you will use.\n(5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List two major early complications of this injury for which you will be monitoring the patient. For each, give a cardinal clinical finding. (4 marks) Complication Cardinal clinical finding"}],"propPages":["asset:img60660a01a518be6879"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Widened joint between base 1st and 2nd metatarsals (Lisfranc injury)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Comminuted fracture base 2nd metatarsal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Transverse fracture mid-shaft 2nd metatarsal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lateral displacement/ dislocation of metatarsals 2-5</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fracture cuboid</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Analgesia – IV morphine 2.5 mg aliquots (or similar)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Short leg backslab</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Elevate and ice</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Orthopaedic referral for ORIF</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Vascular injury</td><td>Loss of pulses – DP pulse</td></tr><tr><td>Compartment syndrome</td><td>Increasing pain, loss sensation and pulses</td></tr><tr><td>(not accept just loss of pulse for this)</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":3,"title":"","stemText":"A 41 year old itinerant alcoholic has been brought to the ED with severe epigastric\nabdominal pain and vomiting. His arterial blood gas, taken on room air, is provided in the PROP BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Comment on the following values on the arterial blood gas. Give likely causes for each abnormality. (8 marks) Electrolyte Comment Likely Cause Sodium Potassium Chloride Calcium"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List other major abnormalities present, and for each list 2 differential diagnoses. (9 Marks) Abnormality Differential Diagnoses"}],"propPages":["asset:imgdb99e38fb3ccdeb659"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Likely GI losses or other cause (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>K 3.5 – corrected for pH =&gt; 2.5 (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>GI losses likely (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>Hypochloremia - ??? (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Vomiting, electrical neutrality (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>Ionised Ca – severe hypocalcaemia (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Pancreatitis, renal failure, rhabdo a possibility (1 mark)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ketones – DKA, alcoholic (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Lactate (type A or B with liver failure) (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>Inadequate respiratory compensation (expect CO2 to be lower) (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Decreased consciousness eg alcohol, head injury, other drugs (1 mark each)</td></tr><tr><td>Delta ratio (42-12)/(22-11) = v. high almost (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>No differential acceptable</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Lactate high</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Type A hypoperfusion (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Type B – liver failure (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>Hyperglycaemia</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>DKA (1 mark)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":4,"title":"","stemText":"A 12 year old boy has been brought to your ED by his parents with profuse blood loss from\nhis mouth. His clothes are covered in blood. He had undergone tonsillectomy at your hospital 7 days prior. On arrival, his vital signs are:\n\nGCS\nPulse 160 /min\n\nBP\n75/40 mmHg\n\nRR\n/min\nO2 saturation 99%\nroom air He looks pale and sweaty. Your initial assessment is that he does not require immediate intubation.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"State your initial management steps, including details of drug doses and end-points. (5 marks) After a brief period of time in the ED, the patient has a large vomit consisting of congealed blood. He appears to aspirate a significant portion of vomitus. You have decided to intubate the patient to protect his airway and to facilitate ongoing management."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Complete the table by listing three difficulties you anticipate in his intubation, and the"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Resuscitate</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>2 IV cannulae – largest possible (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Bolus N/S 20ml/kg acceptable but not essential</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Use O negative blood early – 10ml/kg boluses (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Aim for pulse &lt;120, BP &gt;90 (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">IV Abs – benpen 1.2g (1 mark) +/- metronidazole</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Difficulty</td><td>Solution</td></tr><tr><td>Difficult laryngoscopy due to blood</td><td>Use 2 suction devices</td></tr><tr><td>Haemodynamic collapse on induction</td><td>Extra bolus fluid/blood prior to induction</td></tr><tr><td>Hypoxia during apnoea due to aspiration</td><td>BVM ventilation through induction to</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">minimise apnoea</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">There will be other acceptable alternatives, but don’t be too soft</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":5,"title":"","stemText":"A 25 year old man has been brought to the ED with agitated behaviour. He was found by\nbystanders in a park screaming out “Help!” repeatedly.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List six features of your assessment that would suggest a psychiatric cause of his behavioural disturbance. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________ After a short period of time, the states he is planning to leave. You are considering whether he is competent to make this decision."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List the components of competence assessment. (3 marks) You have decided that the patient is not competent to make competent decisions about his health care in this current state. He is now attempting to leave."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State, in sequential order, your management steps in attempting to keep this patient against his will. Include doses of drugs where appropriate. (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Past history of psychiatric disorder</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Current presentation similar to previous psych presentations</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal vitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">No acute drug intoxication / normal physical examination</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Auditory hallucinations</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Collateral history suggestive of slow onset deterioration</div><div class=\"answer-line\">No medical symptoms / complaints</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Understands current condition</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Understands treatment options</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Understands possible outcomes of each treatment option</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(some assessment of judgement)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Verbal de-escalation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Oral sedation – eg diazepam 10mg po</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Show of force</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Physical restraint</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Parenteral chemical restraint – eg droperidol 10mg IM</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":6,"title":"","stemText":"A 70 year old man has presented to the ED after a syncopal episode.\nHis ECG is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET. The patient’s vital signs are:\n\nGCS\nE3 V4 M5\n\nBP\n70/40 mmHg\n\nRR\n/min O2 sats 99% room air He is pale and sweaty.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Give your diagnosis and supportive ECG findings. (5 marks) Diagnosis: __________________________________________________________________ Supportive Findings:"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State your treatment steps in sequential order. Include details of drug doses and end-points. (10 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:img1511d34d2a431daeab"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">2.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Aiming for BP&gt;90 systolic, pulse &gt;50/min (1 mark)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Sedation midazolam 1mg aliquots until light sedation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pace 60/min</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Chemical pacing – isoprenaline infusion / adrenaline infusion with appropriate doses (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Correct reversible causes – eg hyperK, reperfusion if STEMI becomes apparent on return sinus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">rhythm (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fluid bolus acceptable 500ml N/S, repeat if needed depending on volume assessment (1 mark)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":7,"title":"","stemText":"You are working in an ED in North Queensland. A nine year old child has been brought to the ED by her mother after being bitten by a snake in her backyard. The patient has normal vital signs on arrival.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four specific features of your examination. (4 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List the two most relevant laboratory investigations you will perform. (2 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ Initial assessment demonstrates no evidence of envenomation."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List your discharge criteria for this girl. (5 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Bite site assessment (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lymphadenopathy (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Evidence of bleeding / coagulopathy eg bleeding gums (1)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Evidence of neurotoxicity – peripheral weakness, cranial nerves (max 2 marks for this stuff)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Respiratory assessment – eg PEFR (a mark on its own)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Coag</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CK</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(no other answers)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">12 hours observation (essential to pass)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal examination for neurotoxicity</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal 12 hour coags</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal 12 hour CK</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Parental considerations addressed including access back to hospital if needed (max 2 marks for</div><div class=\"answer-line\">reasonable responses in this domain)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":8,"title":"","stemText":"Your tertiary department has received criticism about its management of ST elevation myocardial infarctions (STEMIs). In particular, there has been concern about time to percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Your director has asked you to implement a protocol for patients with STEMIs.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List the six steps you will take in the development and implementation of this protocol. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List six specific measures that might reduce time to PCI for these patients. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Research current performance numbers – time to PCI, time to ECG, time to senior review etc</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Research benchmarks – ACEM</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Research other department processes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Engage stakeholders – ED, cardiology, ambulance</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Facilitate meeting</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Educate staff about changes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Implement changes</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Audit processes</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Ambulance pre-notification to cardiologist / ED</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Immediate ECG on arrival for all chest pain patients</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Immediate senior doctor review of all ECGs for chest pain</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Single number / point of contact for interventional cardiologist</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Minimise transfers – eg stay on ambulance trolley</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Have transfer packs already made up and ready to use</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early notification wardsmen</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":9,"title":"","stemText":"A 7 day old boy has been brought to your ED with jaundice.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List six causes of neonatal jaundice. For each one, list a cardinal assessment (history, examination or investigation) finding. (12 marks) Cause Cardinal finding"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Distinguishing feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cardinal finding</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>sepsis</td><td>Abnormal vitals – fever, tachycardia</td></tr><tr><td>Breast milk jaundice</td><td>Well child, onset day 3-4</td></tr><tr><td>hypothyroidism</td><td>Abnormal TFTs</td></tr><tr><td>Haematoma breakdown</td><td>Large cephalhaematoma from birth</td></tr><tr><td>Congenital biliary atresia</td><td>Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, pale</td></tr><tr><td>urine/dark stools, day 1 jaundice</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Haemolysis eg ABO incompatibility</td><td>Anaemia, raised LDH, day 1 jaundice</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":10,"title":"","stemText":"A 65 year old man has presented to the ED with shortness of breath.\nHis chest x-ray is provided in the PROP BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List the significant x-ray findings. (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List five specific infectious causes of this pathology. (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List three non-infectious causes of this pathology. (3 marks) The patient’s vital signs are:\n\nGCS\nE4 V5 M6 Pulse 130 /min\n\nBP\n80/40 mmHg Temperature 39.3 degrees\n\nRR\n/min O2 saturations 86% room air"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List your treatment steps in sequential order. Include details of drug doses and end- points where appropriate. (6 marks) After initial therapy, you have decided to insert a central line. You have obtained consent for the procedure."},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List eight key steps in inserting an internal jugular central line. (8 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________ (7)_________________________________________________________________________ (8)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:img984b74203e84fc5225"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">***Fluid level (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">***Surrounding left upper zone consolidation (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Air bronchograms (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Relevant negatives</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>No mediastinal lymphadenopathy (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Solitary area of pathology (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>No effusion (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">*** = critical findings</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Strep pneumoniae</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Staph aureus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Klebsiella pneumoniae</div><div class=\"answer-line\">TB</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fungal – cryptococcus</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Malignancy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Granuloma – eg Wegener’s</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Infarct</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Bronchogenic cyst</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">IV noradrenaline infusion if hypotensive despite IV fluids – 5-40mcg/min as required (1</div><div class=\"answer-line\">mark)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">***Aseptic preparation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">***Head down</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Local anaesthetic to site</div><div class=\"answer-line\">***Use of USS guided technique where possible</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Insert needle to IJV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pass wire</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Dilate vein</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Insert central line</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Flush all lumens</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Secure to skin</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":11,"title":"","stemText":"A 75 year old man has been brought to the ED from his high level care nursing home with\nabdominal pain. His abdominal x-ray is provided in the PROP BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Give your diagnosis, supported by the abnormal radiological findings. (4 marks) Diagnosis:___________________________________________________________________ Radiological Findings:_________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List three possible methods for management of this problem. (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List six considerations when determining the ceiling of care for this gentleman. (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:imgaf850316c54e3020ec"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Massively dilated loop sigmoid colon (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Axis to LIF (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">No rectal gas, proximal dilated large colon (1 mark for either part of this)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">- per rectal deflation – rectal tube or sigmoidoscope</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- laparotomy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">- percutaneous deflation</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Pt wishes if competent</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Advanced health care directive</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Substitute decision maker if pt incompetent</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pt’s quality of life</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Potential reversibility of condition</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Nature of intervention required</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(some other stuff likely to be OK)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":12,"title":"","stemText":"A 37 year old man has been brought to the ED after being found collapsed in the park. It\nwas apparent from his attire that he had been running. His past medical history is unknown. On arrival, his vital signs are:\n\nGCS\nE1 V2 M4 Pulse 130 /min\n\nBP\n80/40 mmHg\n\nRR\n/min O2 saturations 91% room air Temperature 40.1 degrees The working diagnosis is heat stroke.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List six complications of heat stroke. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Give three differential diagnoses. For each, give a cardinal assessment feature. Diagnosis Assessment feature"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Cerebral oedema / decreased GCS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Seizure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulmonary oedema</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Rhabdomyolysis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hyperkalaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Liver failure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cardiogenic shock / cardiomyopathy</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Diagnosis</td><td>Assessment feature</td></tr><tr><td>sepsis</td><td>Meningism, blood cultures</td></tr><tr><td>Drug intoxication eg sympathomimetic</td><td>Dilated pupils, IV track marks</td></tr><tr><td>toxictity</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Thyroid storm</td><td>Raised TFTs</td></tr><tr><td>Other answers likely to be acceptable</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":13,"title":"","stemText":"A 56 year old woman has presented to your rural ED with 1 hour of central chest pain. The\nnearest tertiary centre is 3 hours away.\nHer significant history is of a myocardial infarction 2 years ago. Her ECG is provided in the PROP BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List the pathologies present on the ECG, include the specific ECG features to support your conclusions. (8 marks)\nHer vital signs are:\n\nGCS\n\nBP\n110/60 mmHg\n\nRR\n/min O2 saturations 100% 6L O2 via mask"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State six key treatment steps, including drugs and doses. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:img5d2ad1b66794388b27"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">ST elevation II, III, aVF also V5,6 (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Reciprocal depression aVL, V1 and V2 (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>STE III &gt; II suggestive of RV MI (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">RBBB (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">– broad complex QRS with RSR in V1, normal axis (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Q waves in anterior leads V1-3 (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>suggestive of old anterior MI (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Bold to score =&gt; 0;</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Aspirin 300mg po</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Clopidogrel 300mg / ticagrelor 180mg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Heparin 4000-5000U depending on weight</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV opiate appropriate dose – titrated to pain</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV Tenecteplase – weight based dose if no C/I to thrombolysis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Acknowledgment of RV specific strategies</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":14,"title":"","stemText":"You have been asked to deliver a talk to a group of medical students about paediatric resuscitation.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the following table regarding the specific management of paediatric advanced life support. (6 marks) Topic Details Compression : ventilation rate Adrenaline dose Adrenaline timing (non-shockable rhythm) Amiodarone dose Amiodarone timing Energy setting for defibrillation"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List eight reversible causes to be considered in paediatric advanced life support. (8 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________ (7)_________________________________________________________________________ (8)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Topic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Details</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypoxia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypothermia / hyperthermia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypovolaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hyper/hypokalaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Toxins</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Thrombosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tension PTX</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tamponade</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":15,"title":"","stemText":"A 20 year old man has presented to the ED after falling off a wall on to his right hand.\nThe x-ray of his right forearm is provided in the PROP BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List the abnormalities on the x-ray. Include detailed radiological descriptions of each problem. (8 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"State four management steps for this injury. Include drug doses where appropriate. (5 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img5d21e94cc20cc2b8fb"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Transverse (1)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Displaced medially (1)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Displaced dorsally (1)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Shortened (1)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Volar / anterior angulation (1)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Distal radio-ulnar dislocation (1)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Dorsal displacement ulna (1)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">NOT QUALITY OF XR or MISSING ELBOW as that is not an abnomality</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Analgesia – IV morphine 2.5mg aliquots</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Backslab long arm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Elevation arm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Orthopaedic referral for ORIF (must specify admission / ORIF, not just “ortho referral”)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Qualified reduction in ED – if NV involvement</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":16,"title":"","stemText":"A 36 year old man with a history of schizoaffective disorder has presented following a large\n• verdose of citalopram.\nHis ECG is provided in the PROP BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"In relation to this ECG, complete the following. (3 marks) Rate: Rhythm: Axis:"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What is the main abnormality manifest on the ECG and what does this abnormality represent at a cellular level? (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List three other agents that could produce the same ECG abnormality. (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List six examination features that would be consistent with significant poisoning from citalopram. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:img8d5555620a0fc46ee5"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Sinus rhythm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">75/min</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal axis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Long QT 500-520ms; K ch blockade</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">4.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Serotonin toxicity evidenced by:</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Serotonin toxicity evidenced by:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">hyperthermia, dilated pupils, tremor, hyperreflexia in lower limbs more than upper limbs,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ocular clonus, ankle/finger clonus, tachycardia, seizures</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":17,"title":"","stemText":"A 25 year old woman has presented to the ED with a painful left eye.\nHer clinical photograph is provided in the PROP BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four abnormalities in the photograph. (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What is the likely diagnosis? (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State three important steps in your management of this patient. (4 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List three predisposing conditions for this problem. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img91184c722540561621"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Irregular pupil due to synechiae</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Peri-limbic conjunctival injection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Small hypopyon</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Cloudy cornea (esp inferior half)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Anterior uveitis / iritis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Ophthalmological consultation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Prednisone drop ?corticosteroid drops</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Mydriatic installation eg homatropine / cyclopentolate</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Crohn’s disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">SLE</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sarcoidosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Ank spond</div><div class=\"answer-line\">*(others)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":18,"title":"","stemText":"A 35 year old woman has been brought to the ED with a prolonged generalised seizure.\nHer medical history is unknown.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List four specific pharmacological options for termination of her seizure, including doses. (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List five differential diagnoses for her prolonged seizure. (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List five serious complications of status epilepticus. (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Midazolam 5mg aliquots IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Phenytoin 15mg/kg IV over 30 min</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Levitiracetam 1-2g IV</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Valproate 800-1200mg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Phenobarbitone 10mg/kg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(only one mark for benzos)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Metabolic – hypoglycaemia, hyponatremia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Head injury (of any type)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Encephalitis / meningitis (will not accept “sepsis”)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Drug intoxication eg amphetamines</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intracranial SOL – malignancy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Eclampsia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intracranial haemorrhage spontaneous eg SAH</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Primary epilepsy</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Hypoxic brain injury / cerebral oedema</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intracranial haemorrhage</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulmonary oedema</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Rhabdomyolysis / renal failure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Non-head trauma – eg spine fractures</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(others will be acceptable)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":19,"title":"","stemText":"A 26 year old woman presents with 3 days of lethargy and epigastric pain. She is 28 weeks\npregnant.\nHer vital signs are:\n\nTemp\n37.3 degrees Heart Rate 110 beats per minute Blood Pressure 140/90 mmHg\n\nRR\nbreaths per minute O2 Saturations 95% room air Her blood results demonstrate anaemia with thrombocytopaenia and elevated tranaminases and bilirubin. You suspect that she may have HELLP Syndrome.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three important differential diagnoses for her presentation. (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"In addition to the findings provided, list six examination or investigative findings that would support a diagnosis of HELLP syndrome or pre-eclampsia. (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List five life-threatening complications of pre-eclampsia and HELLP syndrome. (5 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ Whilst being assessed, the patient suffers a generalised tonic-clonic seizure"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Describe your specific management of her seizure. Provide doses of any therapies used and state your clinical end-points (4 marks) The seizure is terminated effectively. The patient’s blood pressure is now 180/110."},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"Describe your key management priorities for this patient. Be specific with any therapies administered. (8 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Sepsis from another focus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intra-uterine foetal demise</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Splenic artery aneurysm</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Signs of impaired cerebral perfusion  - any of Hyperreflexia/Visual</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Disturbance/Mental Status Changes/ clonus (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Headaches</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Vomiting (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Acute Renal Failure with Oliguria (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Elevated urate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pulmonary Oedema</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Evidence of Haemolysis (LDH, schistocytes, decreased haptoglobin) (1 mark)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Placental Abruption (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Acute Renal Failure (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Subcapsular Liver Haematoma (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">***Seizures (1 mark) essential to score &gt;= 0</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intrauterine Fetal Demise (1 mark)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">4. Left lateral position – IVC in pregnancy (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Supportive care – O2 (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Terminate Seizure – midazolam 2.5 - 5mg (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Magnesium 4-6gm loading dose – end point seizure cessation (1 mark) essential</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">a) Prevent further seizures: Magnesium Infusion 1-2 gm/hr</div><div class=\"answer-line\">b) Control Blood Pressure: Hydralazine 5-10mg IV or Labetalol 40mg IV best options</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(avoid oral drugs ie nifedipine and avoid SNP as only 28 weeks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">c) Urgent Obstetric review for consideration of delivery</div><div class=\"answer-line\">d) Betamethasone for lung maturation 12.4gm IMI</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":20,"title":"","stemText":"A 40 year old woman presents to ED complaining of weakness and difficulty walking. On\ninitial assessment she is found to have loss of power in both legs, but normal upper limb and cranial nerve function.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 3 key differential diagnoses you would consider for her presentation. For each\ndifferential, provide supportive historical and/or examination features. (9 marks) Differential diagnosis Supportive Historical and Examination Features You decide to perform a lumbar puncture as part of your workup."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline the key aspects of consent as they apply in this situation. (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>***Is able to make an informed decision without undue influence/ must be freely</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>given (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Also accept:</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Detail around ‘informed’: (1 mark)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Rationale for procedure</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Risks of procedure</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Guillain Barre Syndrome:</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Hx: Ascending weakness, recent GIT illness</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Exam: Areflexia, minimal sensory change</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Multiple Sclerosis:</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Hx MS, other focal neurology (?Optic neuritis)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Exam: UMN signs, incomplete neurology</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Transverse Myelitis:</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Hx: post viral illness</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Exam: Spinal Level</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Functional:</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Inconsistent Hx and exam</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">3. At least:</td></tr><tr><td>***Patient has decision making capacity</td><td>(1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">***Is able to make an informed decision without undue influence/ must be freely</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">given (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Also accept:</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Detail around ‘informed’: (1 mark)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Rationale for procedure</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Risks of procedure</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Risks of not having procedure done</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Any alternatives</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":21,"title":"","stemText":"Your registrar has called you to resus to help with a patient. An 18 year old male presented in diabetic ketoacidosis, complicated by a prolonged generalised seizure. He has been intubated for airway protection and is currently being sedated and mechanically ventilated, awaiting ICU review. His weight is 80kg. A blood gas is provided in the PROP BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the following table for calculations that can be made from the above blood gas. (10 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"a gradient Anion Gap Delta Ratio Corrected Sodium Your registrar asks for guidance to address his respiratory status. Currently the ventilator is set as follows: Mode SIMV-VC\n\nFiO2\n1.0 TV 400 mls Resp Rate breaths/min PEEP cm H2O PSupp cm H2O You notice the patient is making some spontaneous respiratory efforts which are triggering the high pressure alarms."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Give your strategy for improving this patient’s ventilation, stating your desired end- points. (5 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:imgfde62cc1e059ee2a2d"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"4\">this is not a</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">maths test</td></tr><tr><td>Expected</td><td>1.5 x HCO3 + 8</td><td>24.5</td><td>Severe Respiratory acidosis is present</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">CO2</td></tr><tr><td>A-a</td><td>Alveolar O2 =</td><td>Alveolar =</td><td>Marked A-a gradient – implies ineffective</td></tr><tr><td>gradient</td><td>713x 1 – 60/0.8</td><td>640</td><td>gas transfer – shunt/V/Q mismatch</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">gradient =</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">440.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td>Na – (Cl + HCO3)</td><td>28</td><td>HAGMA present (likely ketones given</td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">lactate not too high, could be renal</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">failure or toxic alcohols)</td></tr><tr><td>Delta Gap</td><td>Change in AG/</td><td>28-12/24-</td><td>Pure HAGMA</td></tr><tr><td>change in HCO3</td><td>11 = 16/13</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">= 1</td></tr><tr><td>Corrected</td><td>Na + (Gluc-5)/3</td><td>125 + 10 =</td><td>No hyponatraemia present</td></tr><tr><td>Na+</td><td>135</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">1 mark total for formula and result, 1 mark for clinical implication</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"4\">2. Goals:</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Improve Oxygenation –</div><div class=\"answer-line\">increase PEEP to 10-15cm H20 (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tidal volume to 480-500mls (6mls/kg) (1 mark)</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":22,"title":"","stemText":"You are notified by the public health unit of an impending aircraft arrival from West Africa. A suspected outbreak of Ebola Virus Disease (EVD) amongst health care workers has led to a large scale air evacuation. Your hospital is set to be one of several who will accept arrivals from this flight. The flight is scheduled to arrive in the next few hours.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List and justify the different stakeholders you would engage in your strategy for managing the possible surge of EVD patients. (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List the key principles of managing patients with suspected Ebola Virus Disease in the Emergency Department. (4 marks) You are told that the plane has landed and 10 patients are en route to your hospital. Your department is currently full with many patients awaiting admission or ward bed allocation."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List four strategies you might use to prepare your department for these arrivals. (4 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Hospital Exec – they need to know and will help facilitate flow</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pre-hospital – Ambulance – diversion of non tertiary patients to alternate centres</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">ED groups: Senior Nursing/Triage/Resus – for surge preparedness/capacity issues</div><div class=\"answer-line\">ED Medical Staff – ensure all available staff on duty to meet surge demand</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"empty-state\">The supplied marking resource did not contain extractable text for this part.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":false,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":23,"title":"","stemText":"A 38 year old man presents with rapid palpitations for 30 mins. He feels lightheaded but\ndenies any chest pain. ECG demonstrates a wide-complex tachycardia.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List six ECG findings that would support a diagnosis of Ventricular Tachycardia (VT) (6 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ (6)_________________________________________________________________________"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four features you would seek on history that would support a diagnosis of VT (4 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ You diagnose Ventricular Tachycardia and elect to cardiovert the patient electrically."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State four key steps you would undertake in electrically cardioverting this patient in ED. Be specific with any therapies used. (4 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Fusion beats</div><div class=\"answer-line\">AV dissociation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Extreme Axis (northwest)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Absence of typical LBBB or RBBB morphology</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Very broad QRS</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Concordance – Pos or Neg</div><div class=\"answer-line\">RSR’ with taller left rabbit ear</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Rate appropriate - &gt;120, mostly 150-200</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Others – Josephson’s sign/Brugada’s sign are also ok if explained what they are.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Known Structural Heart Disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Known Ischaemic Heart Disease</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Prior MI</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hx of CCF</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Known cardiomyopathy</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Family Hx of Sudden Cardiac Deat</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">3. Consent</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":24,"title":"","stemText":"An 18 year old girl is brought to ED after a collapse. She has a history of anorexia nervosa. Her ECG is provided in the PROPS BOOKLET.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What is the most important abnormality shown on her ECG? (1 mark)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List one other associated abnormalities present on her ECG (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"State four key investigations you would perform in the ED on this patient. Provide your rationale for each investigation. (8 marks) Investigation Rationale You explain to the patient that she will need to be admitted for further treatment and to address her eating disorder. She tells you she is going to discharge against your advice. Her parents plead with you to keep her in hospital “for her own good”."},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List the conditions would need to be present for you to be able to detain and treat her against her expressed wishes? (4 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":["asset:imgb068b6609de1e43d61"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Long QT</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Left Ventricular Hypertrophy (S-L criteria); U waves; Sinus bradycardia (rate 54/min)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mandatory:</td><td>bHCG***</td></tr><tr><td>BSL***</td><td>Some sort of electrolytes for K and Mg – VBG not enough alone (no Mg)</td></tr><tr><td>Others: FBE (anaemia), TFTs (hypothyroidism), urine (septic screen)</td><td>NEVER PAY PHOSPHATE BECAUSE IT’S NEVER IMPORTANT</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">The patient must be lacking competence to reasonably refuse treatment/ lacking</div><div class=\"answer-line\">capacity</div><div class=\"answer-line\">As demonstrated by a lack of ability to communicate a choice, to understand the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">relevant information, to appreciate the situation and its consequences and to apply</div><div class=\"answer-line\">rational judgement to the available information.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Under these conditions a patient can be kept for treatment and treated in line with</div><div class=\"answer-line\">best medical practice under a duty of care.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">This situation is dynamic.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":25,"title":"","stemText":"A 3 year old boy with normal developmental milestones is brought in by his mother, who\ntells you that he has stopped walking on his right leg for the last 24 hours. On initial examination, the child appears unsettled and will not walk or bear weight on his right leg. You are worried the child may have septic arthritis of the hip joint.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List five important alternate differential diagnoses. (5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List four investigations that may aid in confirming or excluding septic arthritis as the cause of this child’s presentation. Provide rationale for each investigation.(8 marks) Investigation Rationale Your assessment indicates that septic arthritis is unlikely. The child is still not weight bearing but appears well and comfortable."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List five criteria that need to be met to safely discharge this child. (5 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Many here – must have in bold:</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>-</td><td>Infection – psoas abscess, osteomyelitis</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Post infectious – Post- infectious arthritis (ie post salmonella, campylobacter)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Primary Bone – Perthes, SUFE</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":26,"title":"","stemText":"A 50M presents with central chest pain radiating through to his back for 30 minutes. He is sweaty and pale and agitated. He has a background of hypertension for which he has not been treated You are considering a diagnosis of a thoracic aortic dissection.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three specific examination findings that would support a diagnosis of thoracic aortic dissection (3 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ 2. For each of the following investigative modalities, indicate"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Detail your emergency department management of the patient’s haemodynamic state. State your end points. (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Advantage</th><th>Disadvantage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Signs of pericardial effusion</td><td>Evidence of branch dissection</td><td>- carotid/vertebral – neurology</td></tr><tr><td>- blood pressure differential between arms (indicative of brachiocephalic or</td><td>subclav involvement</td><td>- spinal level from spinal infarct (ASA)</td></tr><tr><td>Other – severe hypertension; other risks – Marfans syndrome etc</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">TTE</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Advantages:  repeatable, dynamic, does not require patient to lie flat</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Disadvantages: intolerance (TOE) misses important areas (TTE)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CT(A):</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Advantages: Gives definite diagnosis and aids in repair planning</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":27,"title":"","stemText":"Your registrar has come to see you for help with a patient. They are seeing a 2 year old boy, who was brought to ED by their parent after they fell off the playground equipment at the park, hitting their head.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List five accepted clinical indications for CT Brain imaging for this child.(5 marks) (1)_________________________________________________________________________ (2)_________________________________________________________________________ (3)_________________________________________________________________________ (4)_________________________________________________________________________ (5)_________________________________________________________________________ The child appears well and interactive, with a forehead abrasion but no other injuries. You are told the child had one vomit after the fall, but is now tolerating fluids without intervention. Despite your registrar’s reassurances, the child’s parent is demanding a CT scan."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline the key components of your response to this parent. (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">CHALICE:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">HISTORY: Witnessed LOC &gt;5 mins, Amnesia &gt;5mins, Abnormal Drowsiness, &gt;2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">vomits, Suspected NAI, Traumatic Seizure</div><div class=\"answer-line\">EXAM: GCS &lt;14, suspected Skull injury, Signs of BOS#, Focal Neurology, Bruise&gt;5cm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">MECHANISM: High Speed MVA, Fall &gt;3m, Hit by projectile</div><div class=\"answer-line\">PECARN:</div><div class=\"answer-line\">CATCH: Similar – GCS &lt;15 at 2hrs, Suspected Skull#, Worsening headache, Irritability</div><div class=\"answer-line\">are the HIGH risk features.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Ensure that all relevant communication is clear to ensure no mismatch in</div><div class=\"answer-line\">understanding</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Explain basis of clinical decision – NOT for CT – supported by decision rules</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Identify their concerns re: no CT – explore their concerns; attempt reassurance</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Offer alternate options – SSW for observation etc</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false}]);
